From: Richard Acayan <mailingradian@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Dec 2022 17:45:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soc: qcom: add modemsmem

Modems on some Google Pixel devices require extra information about the
SoC to be passed through a vendor-controlled Shared Memory (SMEM)
channel. This driver provides this information.

Without this driver, the modem might crash with the following message:

    [   17.262410] qcom-q6v5-mss 4080000.remoteproc: fatal error received: :0:[rf]EX:kernel:0:rf:171:PC=d949b6d4
    [   17.262454] remoteproc remoteproc0: crash detected in 4080000.remoteproc: type fatal error

Skimming through the device tree overlays, modemsmem seems to be used by
every Pixel chronologically between the Pixel 3 and Pixel 5a, inclusive.

The modem_flag field is not being initialized yet because the bootloader
doesn't always add the property in dtb (so it might not be necessary)
and because there are problems with how the proper value is being
retrieved in the original downstream driver.

Link: https://android.googlesource.com/kernel/msm/+/6bfa1545e98673df7399a58bfe9a0859b838b715/drivers/soc/qcom/modemsmem/modemsmem.c
Signed-off-by: Richard Acayan <mailingradian@gmail.com>
--
Discussion:

Where do these types of drivers go? This driver is probably providing
info to vendor code in the modem firmware, so it might not belong in
drivers/soc/qcom/.

Maybe it should go in another directory, like:

drivers/misc/
drivers/firmware/

I'm not too familiar with the kernel directory structure, so there might
be somewhere I missed.
--
Original commit:

[WIP] soc: qcom: MODEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEM (add modemsmem)

ok so

the modem normally just needs the firmware, as seen in the OnePlus 6,
SHIFT6mq, and Xiaomi Poco F1

but the Google Pixel 3a and Google Pixel 3 device tree overlays define
another smem client, called "modemsmem"
(https://github.com/LineageOS/android_kernel_google_msm-4.9/blob/lineage-19.1/arch/arm64/boot/dts/google/sdm670-b4s4-common.dtsi#L215
and
https://github.com/LineageOS/android_kernel_google_msm-4.9/blob/lineage-19.1/arch/arm64/boot/dts/google/sdm845-b1c1-common.dtsi#L95)

and even though it does not appear in Caleb's and Amit's Pixel 3 bringup
(https://gitlab.com/sdm845-mainline/linux/-/tree/caleb/pixel3-bringup-next-2022-10-13),
it is still needed to prevent a crash on my Pixel 3a

so the modem can be fed values over smem by the main processor, from the
socinfo driver on the main processor, which fetches values over smem

How silly is that? A modem that doesn't know what hardware is around it,
and needs the software running on the CPU to indirectly fetch values
from what is likely a different part of the device.

Anyway, there are a lot of issues with releasing this:
 - Is the driver in the right subdirectory?
 - Does the driver work on other devices?
